.TH TRANSITPROBE 1 "September 2026" "TransitMon 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
transitprobe \- discover and inspect OBD-II data exposed by a vehicle
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B transitprobe scan
.RI [ --port " DEVICE" ]
.RI [ --output " FILE" ]
.RI [ --mock ]
.PP
.B transitprobe live
.RI [ --port " DEVICE" ]
.RI [ --interval " SECONDS" ]
.RI [ --count " N" ]
.RI [ --mock ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B transitprobe
is the discovery and inspection utility for TransitMon.
It is intended to be run before designing or refining a vehicle-specific dashboard so that the available OBD-II data is measured rather than assumed.
.PP
Version 0.1 uses the standard OBD-II command set exposed by the python-OBD backend.
It does not yet perform Ford-specific enhanced-module discovery, HS-CAN/MS-CAN inventory, or raw CAN capture.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS scan
Connect to the OBD adapter, enumerate the commands reported as supported by the active connection, attempt to read the VIN when VIN access is reported as supported, and write a JSON vehicle profile.
.PP
The scan output includes the effective adapter port, detected protocol name, VIN when available, supported-command count, and a command listing.
.TP
.BI --port " DEVICE"
Use the specified serial device, for example
.IR /dev/ttyUSB0 .
If omitted, the backend attempts serial-port auto-detection.
.TP
.BI --output " FILE"
Write the vehicle profile to
.IR FILE .
The default is
.IR ~/.local/share/transitmon/vehicle-profile.json .
.TP
.B --mock
Use the built-in mock vehicle instead of physical OBD hardware.
.SS live
Display live values for common dashboard commands that the connected vehicle reports as supported.
The display is refreshed repeatedly until interrupted or until the requested update count is reached.
.TP
.BI --port " DEVICE"
Use the specified serial device, for example
.IR /dev/ttyUSB0 .
If omitted, the backend attempts serial-port auto-detection.
.TP
.BI --interval " SECONDS"
Set the delay between display updates.
The default is 1.0 second.
Values lower than 0.1 seconds are clamped to 0.1 seconds.
.TP
.BI --count " N"
Stop after
.I N
updates.
The default is 0, which means continue until Ctrl-C.
.TP
.B --mock
Use the built-in mock vehicle instead of physical OBD hardware.
.SH VEHICLE PROFILE
The default profile path is:
.PP
.nf
~/.local/share/transitmon/vehicle-profile.json
.fi
.PP
The JSON profile contains a schema version, creation timestamp, adapter port, protocol, VIN when available, and the supported-command inventory discovered during the scan.
.PP
The profile is intended to provide a reproducible record of what the vehicle actually exposed at scan time.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan a USB adapter on
.IR /dev/ttyUSB0 :
.PP
.nf
transitprobe scan --port /dev/ttyUSB0
.fi
.PP
Write the profile to a custom path:
.PP
.nf
transitprobe scan --port /dev/ttyUSB0 --output ./transit-profile.json
.fi
.PP
Watch live supported values once per second:
.PP
.nf
transitprobe live --port /dev/ttyUSB0
.fi
.PP
Take ten updates at half-second intervals:
.PP
.nf
transitprobe live --port /dev/ttyUSB0 --interval 0.5 --count 10
.fi
.PP
Exercise discovery without hardware:
.PP
.nf
transitprobe scan --mock
transitprobe live --mock --count 5
.fi
.SH SERIAL PERMISSIONS
On Debian systems, USB serial access commonly requires membership in the
.B dialout
group.
For example:
.PP
.nf
sudo usermod -aG dialout "$USER"
.fi
.PP
Log out and back in after changing group membership.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful scan, normal live-mode completion, or live mode interrupted with Ctrl-C.
.TP
.B 1
The OBD adapter could not be opened or connected, or another operating-system-level connection error occurred.
.TP
.B 2
No common live dashboard commands were reported as supported, or invalid command-line usage was supplied to the argument parser.
.SH NOTES
A command being defined by OBD-II does not mean that a particular vehicle implements it.
The purpose of
.B transitprobe
is to record the commands actually reported by the connected vehicle.
.PP
Use the resulting inventory to decide which values should appear in
.BR transitmon (1)
rather than assuming that a sensor or PID exists.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR transitmon (1)
.SH AUTHORS
Rory Hinkel and contributors.
